<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/foundational/ethics-three-theories">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    What makes an action right? Philosophy's three great answers look at three different things.
    <strong>Consequentialism</strong> looks at outcomes: count the good produced. <strong>Deontology</strong>
    looks at duties and rules: some acts are forbidden regardless of results. <strong>Virtue
    ethics</strong> looks at character: the right act is what a practically wise, honest, brave
    person would do. Most everyday moral instincts borrow from all three.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the ethics conversation, twenty-five centuries long — filter by era" widget="timeline" config={ethTimeline} />

  <h2>One case, three verdicts</h2>
  <p>
    Take lying. A utilitarian asks: does this lie produce more wellbeing than honesty? (Usually
    no — trust is precious — but the Nazi-at-the-door case passes.) A Kantian refuses outright:
    a lie treats the deceived person as a tool, and universalised lying destroys language's
    point. A virtue ethicist asks what the truthful person would do — and finds a third answer:
    not lying, but also not volunteering everything; tact is a virtue too.
  </p>

  <h2>Where each theory hurts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Utilitarianism</strong> can justify sacrificing an innocent for the majority — the trolley problem (Philippa Foot, 1967) is designed to make that cost visible.</li>
    <li><strong>Kantian duty</strong> can conflict: never lie vs protect the innocent. Kant's own tangled answer to that case suggests the theory strains.</li>
    <li><strong>Virtue ethics</strong> can feel circular — "the honest act is what the virtuous do, and the virtuous do honest acts" — until you add human flourishing as the anchor.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The professional takeaway is not to pick a team but to <em>name the framework</em> you're
    using when you judge — most public moral arguments are people in different frameworks
    shouting past each other.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Aristotle, Nicomachean Ethics; Kant, Groundwork (1785); Mill, Utilitarianism (1863); Foot, "The Problem of Abortion and the Doctrine of Double Effect" (1967).</p>
